WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.700
Transfer is the ability to use learning outside the original lesson.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.568
Near transfer happens when the new situation closely resembles the practice activity.

00:00:07.568 --> 00:00:11.520
Far transfer requires the learner to adapt the principle to a different context.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.325
Both matter, but real work often demands more than repeating the same example.

00:00:15.325 --> 00:00:18.790
Conditions are messy, information is incomplete, priorities compete,

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.710
and the learner has to decide what applies.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:23.830
Courses support transfer when they include realistic scenarios,

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.470
meaningful decision points, and context variation.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.285
Instead of showing only one perfect example,

00:00:28.285 --> 00:00:31.400
the learning experience can show several versions of the problem.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.080
What changes when the data is incomplete?

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.755
What changes when time is limited?

00:00:34.755 --> 00:00:37.040
What signal tells the learner which method to use?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.190
Variation helps learners recognize the underlying pattern rather than memorizing the

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.120
surface details of a single exercise.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.450
Job aids, checklists, examples, and workflow references can also support transfer.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.090
They are not a sign that learning failed.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.750
They are part of good performance design, especially for tasks that are complex,

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.045
infrequent, safety-sensitive, or easy to confuse under pressure.

00:00:56.045 --> 00:00:59.630
The point is to help people use what they learned when the course is no longer open and

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.760
the work has to be done in the real environment.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.060
A strong transfer question is,

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.110
where will this learner first need the skill after the lesson ends?

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.240
That question changes design decisions.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.330
It affects examples, practice conditions, language, timing, and support materials.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.710
If the first real use happens during a busy shift, a complex review,

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.210
or a handoff between teams,

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:21.115
the course should prepare learners for that context instead of only preparing them for a

00:01:21.115 --> 00:01:22.400
clean classroom version.
